WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Renewed US and Iranian strikes have ended a law in their conflict, raising immediate risks for energy supplies and stability across the Middle East. This is Omni View, episode 163. The United States and Iran carried out new attacks on each other's positions in the Middle East. Reports indicate the exchanges followed earlier strikes and marked the end of a temporary pause in open hostilities. Iran attacked US bases after reports that a US strike killed five people at a wedding

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.720
party. The Iranian red crescent says a child was among those killed when shrapnel from a US strike hit a home. The US military says it is aware of the reports and it never targets civilians. Reuters frames the resumption as a direct response cycle between the two governments, noting specific targets hit on each side. BBC reporting highlights Iranian statements that a US strike affected civilians, while US military sources state they do not target non-combatants.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:25.440
Those two outlets draw on official statements from the involved parties rather than independent verification of all claims. Strikes took place, that much is established. Intent and civilian impact are where the accounts diverge. Iran's public case, as the BBC reports it, is that a US strike reached a wedding, killed five people including a child, and that hitting US bases was the necessary response to that harm.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:44.580
The BBC is drawing on Iranian and red crescent statements that have not been fully independently verified in public reporting. The US military's account, as Reuters records it, is that US forces never target civilians, and that these exchanges sit inside an ongoing regional conflict aimed at military positions.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:59.520
That statement describes targeting policy. It does not, by itself, resolve the red crescent's casualty claims. The fighting comes amid ongoing regional tensions tied to the broader conflict, and it tests whether any de-escalation track can hold.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.440
Energy supplies and stability across the Middle East are immediately at risk because the law is over.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:14.940
Coming days will turn on the mix of targets and language, military or symbolic sites, and statements that either close off further rounds or leave them available.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:23.340
Across the Pacific, the argument turns on whether Taiwan's seat at a regional table is ordinary diplomacy or a challenge to sovereignty.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:29.420
Taiwan's foreign minister described Chinese behavior as bullying during a regional meeting in the Pacific.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:34.720
The statement came as Pacific nation's discussed cooperation and external influence.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:41.060
The remarks address Chinese actions that Taiwan views as attempts to limit its diplomatic participation.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:48.360
Nikkeh Asia presents those remarks as part of Taiwan's effort to maintain diplomatic space amid pressure from Beijing.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:55.920
Chinese officials have consistently viewed Taiwan's participation in such forums as a challenge to their position on sovereignty.

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:09.620
Taiwan did raise the issue publicly at the gathering. That fact is not indisputed. For Taipei, speaking up in a room full of Pacific governments is how you keep diplomatic space when a larger neighbor tries to shrink it.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:20.460
Officials who take that view still have to work in a setting where multiple Pacific states are weighing their own relations with larger powers, which limits how far anyone's speech can move the room.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:29.680
For Beijing, Taiwan's presence at those forums is not a small procedural matter. It is treated as a direct challenge to China's position on sovereignty.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:39.720
Taiwan's strategy of highlighting perceived pressure points will now play out as Pacific governments decide how they handle invitations, seating, and future forums.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:50.880
Next, Chinese leaders Xi Jinping outlined plans for an artificial intelligence or AI center during a gathering of the Shanghai Cooperation Organization, or SCO.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.600
The group presented itself as a source of regional stability.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:04:01.260
The proposal links technology development to the organization's broader security and economic coordination goals.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:08.520
Nike Aja reports the pitch as an attempt to expand China's role in technology governance inside the SCO framework.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:14.780
Member states have varying levels of alignment with Beijing on technology standards and security cooperation.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:21.120
The presentation ties the AI hub to broader claims of providing an alternative to Western led arrangements.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:31.020
SCO members include countries with differing priorities on data sharing and infrastructure standards, and that mix will shape how far the initiative actually advances.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:38.460
Xi Jinping's framing is that an AI hub inside this grouping can serve stability and coordination at the same time.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:47.660
Governments less aligned with Beijing on technology standards may still sit in the room while moving slowly on data sharing and common infrastructure rules.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:53.500
Nike Aja's coverage treats the hub as a governance play, not only a research lab.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:05:00.660
How the proposal is written down and which members put money and data into it will tell you more than the announcement itself.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:12.180
In Nigeria, defense officials stated that security operations over two months resulted in more than 1,000 militants killed and 777 people freed from captivity.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.920
The figures were released during a briefing at the National Counterterrorism Center.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.400
Defense spokesperson Samila Uba disclosed the numbers on Tuesday.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:27.440
All Africa draws on that spokesperson's account of operations conducted across multiple areas.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:35.460
Premium Times summarized the same security briefing, reporting the official totals for militants killed and kidnapped victims rescued.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.000
The announcement emphasizes measurable results in counterterrorism efforts.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:46.200
Independent verification of the exact casualty and rescue numbers remains limited in public reporting.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:52.060
The operations targeted groups involved in kidnapping and attacks in northern and central regions.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:56.700
Those totals come from a national briefing, not from a third party count.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:04.500
Further briefings from the National Counterterrorism Center and any independent reporting that can check the figures are the next place to look.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:06.500
Shifting now to industry.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:14.680
Sony's semiconductor unit has announced a collaboration with Saudi Aramco to develop AI tools for monitoring industrial facilities.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:18.800
The project focuses on predictive maintenance and energy infrastructure.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:24.780
The partnership applies semiconductor technology to large-scale oil and gas operations.

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:31.520
Nikkeh Asia describes the agreement as an expansion of Japanese technology into Gulf energy operations.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:36.600
Saudi Aramco has pursued digital upgrades to its facilities in recent years.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:41.840
Aramco's interest centers on reducing downtime through data-driven monitoring systems.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:45.840
The partnership links chip capabilities with plant-level monitoring,

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.660
with the aim of spotting trouble before a facility has to stop.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:54.940
Work now moves from announcement, input building, and testing those tools on Aramco sites.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:07:01.340
In markets, a Hong Kong listing upgrade has given Baidu a clearer path to mainland Chinese capital.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:05.460
The change follows existing rules on cross-border capital movement.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:11.300
The upgrade allows the company to tap domestic investor pools while maintaining its Hong Kong presence.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:18.880
Nikkeh Asia reports the upgrade as a step that could increase liquidity for the company while staying within regulatory channels.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:25.280
Chinese technology firms have used Hong Kong listings to balance domestic and international investor access.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.840
The move reflects ongoing adjustments in how listed companies navigate capital controls.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:37.440
Baidu's action aligns with similar steps taken by other major platforms seeking additional funding sources.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:45.080
Liquidity and whether mainland capital actually flows through the new channel will show whether the upgrade changes much in practice.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:47.920
Now, some progress worth knowing about.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:57.020
The United Nations or UN Climate Fund has asked Asian countries to increase financial support after the United States withdrew its pledges.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:02.200
The request seeks to maintain funding levels for adaptation and mitigation projects.

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:06.400
The appeal comes as the fund continues operations in multiple regions.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:12.480
Nikkeh Asia frames the appeal as a direct response to the funding gap created by the US decision.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:21.460
Asian governments vary in their willingness and capacity to fill that gap with some already major contributors and others focused on domestic priorities.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:25.120
The fund continues to operate while seeking new commitments.

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:31.200
The request targets both developed and emerging economies in the region to sustain project pipelines.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:37.620
Fresh pledges and how fast money reaches existing projects are the practical test of this appeal.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:42.400
Now, to really understand this story, there's something most coverage leaves out.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:48.180
Most reporting on renewed US Iran strikes treats each round as simple retaliation.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:54.940
The pattern, though, shows deliberate choices about targets and timing that both governments use to signal limits.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:09:01.300
In practice, strikes often focus on military or symbolic sites rather than population centers.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:07.320
Public statements emphasize defensive intent while avoiding language that commits to open-ended campaigns.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:17.520
This approach draws from decades of precedent in which each actor calculates that a measured response preserves room for later de-escalation without appearing weak at home.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:31.200
Historical cases such as the 1980s tanker war and later limited exchanges illustrate how both governments have repeatedly chosen actions that inflict costs but stop short of triggering full mobilization by the other.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:42.600
Knowing that when a report describes a strike as a major escalation, you can check whether the chosen targets and the accompanying statements align with past patterns of restraint or break from them.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:51.420
A practical thing to look for in the next official statements is whether either government rules out another round or pointedly leaves that choice open.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:59.600
Public claims about civilian impact are often issued quickly by one government and met with denials or investigations by the other.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:04.840
That cycle requires cross-checking multiple official channels before conclusions form.

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:13.140
Cross-reference official statements from all involved governments with reporting from multiple regions rather than relying on any single outlet.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:20.500
Primary documents such as Ministry releases and fund reports provide the clearest baseline for claims about actions taken.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:27.900
If you want to go deeper on the renewed U.S. Iran exchanges, compare how Reuters and the BBC are covering them.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:30.960
You will see two different frames on the same facts.

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:37.420
Tomorrow, watch for how Asian governments answer the United Nations Climate Fund's request for more support.

